 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+### Limiting the number of targets per job
+
+A misbehaving service discovery source (a cloud API, an orchestrator, a catalog, etc.) can occasionally return
+a huge number of targets for a single `job_name`, for example because a tag filter was accidentally removed
+or a catalog query became too broad. Scraping all of them at once can spike CPU and memory usage on the `vmagent`
+instance and degrade scraping for every other job running on it.
+
+The `max_scrape_targets` option at the given `scrape_config` (or the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line
+flag for a global default) limits the number of active scrape targets a single `job_name` can have at once,
+regardless of which service discovery mechanism (or combination of mechanisms) supplied the targets. By default,
+`max_scrape_targets` isn't set, i.e. the number of active scrape targets per job is unlimited, so existing setups
+are unaffected. For example, the following config limits the `foo` job to at most `1000` active targets:
+
+```yaml
+scrape_configs:
+- job_name: foo
+  max_scrape_targets: 1000
+  kubernetes_sd_configs:
+  - role: pod
+```
+
+Targets discovered above the limit aren't scraped. They are shown as `DROPPED (job target limit)` targets
+at the `http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` page and in the `droppedTargets` list returned
+by the `http://vmagent:8429/api/v1/targets` API, so operators can notice and fix the misbehaving discovery source
+using the same places they already use for troubleshooting scrape targets. The `(<active>/<total> active)` counter
+shown for the job on the `/service-discovery` page reflects how many targets are currently being excluded
+because of the limit.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
